EventEmitter And Events

1. What is EventEmitter?
    EventEmitter is a Node.js class that allows an object to:
        Emit an event
        Listen for an event
        Execute a callback when that event occurs
    
    const EventEmitter = require("events");
    const emitter = new EventEmitter();

    emitter.on("login", (username) => {
        console.log(`${username} logged in`);
    });

    emitter.emit("login", "Dharitri");

    Output:
        Dharitri logged in

    The basic pattern is:
        emit("event")
            ↓
        listeners
            ↓
        callbacks execute


2. .on() — Listen to an event
    emitter.on("orderCreated", (order) => {
        console.log("Order created:", order);
    });

    Every time the event is emitted:

    emitter.emit("orderCreated", 101);
    emitter.emit("orderCreated", 102);

    The listener runs twice.


3. .once() — Listen only once
    emitter.once("connected", () => {
        console.log("Connected");
    });

    If:
        emitter.emit("connected");
        emitter.emit("connected");
    Output:
        Connected
    The listener is automatically removed after the first execution.

    .on() vs .once()?
        .on() keeps listening for every occurrence of an event, while .once() automatically removes the listener after the first occurrence.


4. .emit()
    Used to trigger an event:
        emitter.emit("userRegistered", user);

    You can pass arguments:
        emitter.on("userRegistered", (name, email) => {
            console.log(name);
            console.log(email);
        });
        emitter.emit(
            "userRegistered",
            "John",
            "john@example.com"
        );


5. .off() / .removeListener()
    You can remove a listener.
        function handleLogin(user) {
            console.log(user);
        }

        emitter.on("login", handleLogin);

        emitter.off("login", handleLogin);

    Equivalent older API:
        emitter.removeListener("login", handleLogin);

    Important: you need the same function reference to remove the listener.


6. Practical backend example
    Imagine a user registration API:
        POST /register
            ↓
        Create user
            ↓
        emit("userRegistered")
            ↓
        ┌──────────────┬──────────────┐
        ↓              ↓              ↓
        Send email   Analytics     Audit log

    Implementation:
        const EventEmitter = require("events");

        const events = new EventEmitter();

        events.on("userRegistered", (user) => {
            console.log("Send welcome email:", user.email);
        });

        events.on("userRegistered", (user) => {
            console.log("Track registration:", user.id);
        });

        function registerUser(user) {
            // save user to database...

            events.emit("userRegistered", user);
        }

    This creates loose coupling between the registration logic and listeners.


7. But EventEmitter is NOT a message queue
    EventEmitter works inside the same Node.js process.
        Node Process
        ├── API
        ├── EventEmitter
        └── Listeners
    If your application crashes, those in-memory events are gone.
    For distributed systems, you might use:
        RabbitMQ
        Kafka
        AWS SQS
        Redis Streams
    Conceptually:
        Service A
        ↓
        Message Broker
        ↓
        Service B

    EventEmitter is an in-process event mechanism. It is useful for decoupling components within a Node.js application, but it isn't a durable distributed messaging system like Kafka or RabbitMQ.


8. Important: EventEmitter listeners are synchronous
    emitter.on("test", () => {
        console.log("Listener");
    });

    console.log("Before");
    emitter.emit("test");
    console.log("After");

    Output:
        Before
        Listener
        After

    emit() invokes registered listeners synchronously.
    This means a CPU-heavy listener can block the event loop.
        emitter.on("test", () => {
            // huge CPU-heavy loop
        });

    Don't assume:
        It's an event, therefore it's asynchronous.
    
    Events and asynchronous execution are separate concepts.


9. Error events
    If an EventEmitter emits an "error" event and there is no error listener, Node can terminate the process.
        emitter.on("error", (err) => {
            console.error("Something went wrong:", err);
        });

        emitter.emit("error", new Error("Database failed"));

    So for custom EventEmitters, handle "error" when errors may be emitted.


10. Memory leak warning
    Suppose you repeatedly add listeners:
        emitter.on("data", handler);

    without removing them.
    Eventually you can have:
        Event
        ↓
        Listener 1
        Listener 2
        Listener 3
        ...
        Listener 100

    This can cause unnecessary memory usage and Node may warn about too many listeners.
    Common causes:
        registering listeners inside frequently called functions
        forgetting to remove listeners
        repeatedly creating subscriptions

    Q. Your Node application shows a MaxListenersExceededWarning. What would you investigate?
        I'd check whether listeners are being registered repeatedly without cleanup, 
        especially inside request handlers, loops, or long-lived subscriptions. 
        I wouldn't simply increase the listener limit without identifying the underlying leak.


11. EventEmitter vs Promises
    Promise:
        Represents the result of one asynchronous operation.
            const result = await getUser();
    
    EventEmitter:
        Represents multiple occurrences of events over time.
            emitter.on("message", handler);
    
    Think:
        Promise:
            One result
                ↓
            resolve / reject

        EventEmitter:
            Many events
                ↓
            event → event → event → event


12. EventEmitter vs callback
    Callback:
        getUser((err, user) => {
            // one callback
        });

    EventEmitter:
        socket.on("data", chunk => {
            // potentially many times
        });

    So:
        Callback → generally one result/callback execution
        EventEmitter → potentially many events over time


13. Real Node.js examples
    HTTP server:
        server.on("request", handler);
    
    Streams:
        stream.on("data", handler);
        stream.on("end", handler);
    
    Socket:
        socket.on("data", handler);
        socket.on("close", handler);

    Node uses this event-driven pattern heavily.


14. Most important interview questions
    Q1. What is EventEmitter?
        EventEmitter is a Node.js mechanism for implementing event-driven behavior where objects can emit named events and other parts of the application can listen for them.

    Q2. How do you create an EventEmitter?
        const EventEmitter = require("events");
        const emitter = new EventEmitter();

    Q3. on() vs once()?
        on() handles every occurrence; once() handles only the first occurrence.

    Q4. Is EventEmitter asynchronous?
        Not inherently. EventEmitter invokes listeners synchronously when emit() is called. The listener itself can start asynchronous work.

    Q5. EventEmitter vs message broker?
        EventEmitter is in-process and in-memory; message brokers provide mechanisms for communication between processes/services and can provide durability and delivery guarantees depending on the system.

    Q6. What happens if an "error" event has no listener?
        An unhandled EventEmitter error event can cause the Node.js process to throw and terminate.



Remember this
EventEmitter
    │
    ├── on()       → listen repeatedly
    ├── once()     → listen once
    ├── emit()     → trigger event
    ├── off()      → remove listener
    └── error      → handle emitter errors

Key interview trap:
    Event-driven does not mean asynchronous. emit() calls listeners synchronously unless you explicitly introduce asynchronous behavior.
